{"causes": ["The intellectual ferment and theological disputes between various schools of thought, including Mu'tazilites and traditionalists, necessitated a new systematic approach to Islamic doctrine.", "The perceived excesses of Mu'tazilite rationalism, which some viewed as undermining divine attributes and scriptural literalism, created a demand for a balanced theological alternative.", "The need for a robust intellectual defense of traditional Sunni beliefs against philosophical challenges and sectarian innovations spurred the development of a sophisticated kalam system."], "effects": ["The establishment of the Ash'ari school became the dominant theological tradition in Sunni Islam, providing a coherent framework for understanding God, revelation, and human agency.", "Al-Ash'ari's synthesis integrated rational inquiry with scriptural authority, allowing Sunni scholars to engage with philosophical questions while upholding religious orthodoxy.", "His work laid the groundwork for future Islamic theologians, most notably al-Ghazali, who further solidified Ash'arism's intellectual and spiritual prominence across the Islamic world."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "mutazilites", "sourceName": "Mu'tazilites", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "abu-al-hasan-al-ashari", "targetName": "Abu al-Hasan al-Ash'ari", "context": "Abu al-Hasan al-Ash'ari was initially a prominent student of the Mu'tazilite scholar Abu Ali al-Jubba'i, mastering their rationalist methodology before his eventual departure from the school around 912 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "abu-al-hasan-al-ashari", "sourceName": "Abu al-Hasan al-Ash'ari", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "baghdad", "targetName": "Baghdad, Iraq", "context": "Abu al-Hasan al-Ash'ari spent his later and most intellectually productive years in Baghdad, a major center of Islamic scholarship, where he refined and propagated his theological system until his death in 935 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "abu-al-hasan-al-ashari", "sourceName": "A